      "math_academy_id": "q-94453",
      "sequence_position": 73,
      "problem": "A data set has mean $\\overset{―}{x}=17$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=4.06.$ Find the $z$-score of an observation $x=12.3,$ giving your answer to $2$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "The $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the data set's mean.\n\nHere, we're told that the mean is $\\overset{―}{x}=17,$ and we can calculate the standard deviation as\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{4.06}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=12.3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & ≈\\frac{12.3-17}{\\sqrt{4.06}} \\\\  & ≈-2.33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places.",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
